I began testing a lagged VIX-conditioned residual reversal: three-day returns are volatility-scaled, cross-sectionally demeaned, inverted, and multiplied by a prior-day VIX percentile stress weight. The initial script exposed a pandas alignment bug in the residual demeaning step, so no valid performance result was produced yet. No factor was persisted or modified; the existing library remains unchanged.The test setup uses only completed historical observations and next-day returns across the 15 tradable instruments. I am correcting the alignment issue before interpreting results, rather than treating the malformed zero-row output as a validation result.The specific bug is that row-wise cross-sectional demeaning was subtracted with the wrong pandas axis, creating a date-by-date expanded object instead of a 15-column panel. I will rerun after changing the subtraction to align the row means by index.No signal artifact from the malformed run will be used for admission.Next action: fix the residual construction, rerun IC/ICIR, regime, and decay diagnostics through the current data horizon, then retain the candidate only if it clears both gates and has acceptable overlap with the existing stress-reversal library.I’m applying the alignment correction now.The correction is straightforward: use `r3.sub(r3.mean(axis=1), axis=0)` so each date’s cross-sectional mean aligns to the corresponding date row.## Current Step Summary

